DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The SHA_ and SHA1_ functions calculate a 160-bit cryptographic checksum
     (digest) for any number of input bytes.  A cryptographic checksum is a
     one-way hash function; that is, it is computationally impractical to find
     the input corresponding to a particular output.  This net result is a
     “fingerprint” of the input-data, which does not disclose the actual in‐
     put.

     SHA (or SHA-0) is the original Secure Hash Algorithm specified in FIPS
     160.  It was quickly proven insecure, and has been superseded by SHA-1.
     SHA-0 is included for compatibility purposes only.

     The SSHHAA11__IInniitt(), SSHHAA11__UUppddaattee(), and SSHHAA11__FFiinnaall() functions are the core
     functions.  Allocate an _S_H_A___C_T_X, initialize it with SSHHAA11__IInniitt(), run over
     the data with SSHHAA11__UUppddaattee(), and finally extract the result using
     SSHHAA11__FFiinnaall(), which will also erase the _S_H_A___C_T_X.

     SSHHAA11__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSHHAA11__FFiinnaall() which converts the return value
     to a 41-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII string which
     represents the 160 bits in hexadecimal.

     SSHHAA11__FFiillee() calculates the digest of a file, and uses SSHHAA11__EEnndd() to re‐
     turn the result.  If the file cannot be opened, a null pointer is re‐
     turned.  SSHHAA11__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() is similar to SSHHAA11__FFiillee(), but it only calcu‐
     lates the digest over a byte-range of the file specified, starting at
     _o_f_f_s_e_t and spanning _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes.  If the _l_e_n_g_t_h parameter is specified
     as 0, or more than the length of the remaining part of the file,
     SSHHAA11__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() calculates the digest from _o_f_f_s_e_t to the end of file.
     SSHHAA11__DDaattaa() calculates the digest of a chunk of data in memory, and uses
     SSHHAA11__EEnndd() to return the result.

     When using SSHHAA11__EEnndd(), SSHHAA11__FFiillee(), or SSHHAA11__DDaattaa(), the _b_u_f argument can
     be a null pointer, in which case the returned string is allocated with
     malloc(3) and subsequently must be explicitly deallocated using free(3)
     after use.  If the _b_u_f argument is non-null it must point to at least 41
     characters of buffer space.

EERRRROORRSS
     The SSHHAA11__EEnndd() function called with a null buf argument may fail and re‐
     turn NULL if:

     [ENOMEM]           Insufficient storage space is available.

     The SSHHAA11__FFiillee() and SSHHAA11__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() may return NULL when underlying
     open(2), fstat(2), lseek(2), or SHA1_End(3) fail.

